# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever checked the repository out.
#
# Without a rule the answer is each machine's core.autocrlf, and this project is
# built on three of them: Windows says CRLF, the Mac and Linux say nothing. Two
# sample files already reached the history with carriage returns in them that
# way. So the repository states it once, for every machine, and a checkout is
# the same bytes wherever it happens.
#
# LF rather than "whatever the platform prefers", for three reasons this
# project has already paid for:
#
#  - tool/installer/install.sh is copied into a release as it is, and run by
#    `sh` on a machine that has nothing else. With CRLF endings the shell reads
#    the carriage return as part of the last word on the line and refuses
#    `set -eu` as an illegal option — a release that built cleanly on the build
#    machine and is broken on the target one.
#  - The outline goldens under test/data are compared byte for byte against
#    text the test builds with '\n'. A checkout that turns them into CRLF fails
#    eleven tests with a diff that shows two identical-looking trees, which is
#    how this was found on Linux — and the same checkout does it on Windows.
#  - Nothing in the build minds. Dart, CMake, MSVC, the resource compiler and
#    Python all read LF, and so does every editor written this century.
#
# text=auto is kept alongside eol=lf so files git detects as binary — the
# pictures, the icons, the sample archives — are still left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell reads either, but a file Notepad opens is nicer with CRLF, and
# install.ps1 is the one a person might have to look at on a machine with
# nothing installed.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
